<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-02-05.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="004923"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004923" label="[00:49:23]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: Oh, interesting, didn't even consider that....</span></div><div class="line" id="005011"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005011" label="[00:50:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: huh, no idea how CI passed then....</span></div><div class="line" id="005042"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005042" label="[00:50:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: what is the version of python there?</span></div><div class="line" id="005042"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#005042" label="[00:50:42]">&lt;snuik&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, you have 1 message</span></div><div class="line" id="005042"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#005042" label="[00:50:42]">&lt;snuik&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, janneke says: oh that's great news!</span></div><div class="line" id="005057"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005057" label="[00:50:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, yeah, we updated to 24.04</span></div><div class="line" id="005057"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005057" label="[00:50:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, that's old news from janneke, I saw that...</span></div><div class="line" id="005115"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005115" label="[00:51:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so on my Gentoo system I tested, it starts working from 3.12</span></div><div class="line" id="005124"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005124" label="[00:51:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and they indeed have changes to f-string in changelong</span></div><div class="line" id="005134"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005134" label="[00:51:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in particular now you can have arbitrary nesting of f-strings</span></div><div class="line" id="005216"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#005216" label="[00:52:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">before 3.12 this was the deepest possible nesting f&quot;&quot;&quot;{f'''{f'{f&quot;{1+1}&quot;}'}'''}&quot;&quot;&quot; (limited by the number of different quotes)</span></div><div class="line" id="005218"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005218" label="[00:52:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">didn't even know that limitation existed, when i was writing the code i just thought this should work... right...</span></div><div class="line" id="005220"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005220" label="[00:52:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it did</span></div><div class="line" id="005228"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005228" label="[00:52:28]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but apparently only 3.12+</span></div><div class="line" id="010019"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010019" label="[01:00:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: oh you had to completely rewrite some generators for gcc pregen files...</span></div><div class="line" id="010234"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010234" label="[01:02:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, looks reasonable</span></div><div class="line" id="010250"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010250" label="[01:02:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and at this stage I would probably trust your problematic-source more than myself looking at pregen-files...</span></div><div class="line" id="010344"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010344" label="[01:03:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">me too, i think i overestimated my ability to find pregen files lol, heuristics work better than i expected</span></div><div class="line" id="010350"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010350" label="[01:03:50]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, just one generator needed rewriting</span></div><div class="line" id="010408"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010408" label="[01:04:08]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">has a very obtuse history, was published in a research paper with a singular implementation</span></div><div class="line" id="010420"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010420" label="[01:04:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">clearly someone ran it, checked it into version control, and left it since</span></div><div class="line" id="010542"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010542" label="[01:05:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah...</span></div><div class="line" id="010608"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010608" label="[01:06:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you can always try to upstream it :)</span></div><div class="line" id="010624"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010624" label="[01:06:24]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah solid idea</span></div><div class="line" id="010628"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010628" label="[01:06:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not sure whether they care about pregen files though</span></div><div class="line" id="010644"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#010644" label="[01:06:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as we saw, some maintainers don't really understand the concept</span></div><div class="line" id="010734"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010734" label="[01:07:34]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah.... often i see that they will have the generators in-tree, but they also distribute the generated file</span></div><div class="line" id="010756"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#010756" label="[01:07:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is one of the few cases where the generator just.. isn't maintained or existing</span></div><div class="line" id="023253"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023253" label="[02:32:53]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy, stikonas: before python 3.12, f-strings were strings with specially parsed contents. After 3.12, f-strings enter a new lexer context every time you transition across quote characters, and determining the end-of-string position requires a PEG parser running the cpython grammar so that it can evaluate whether a quote character is part of an embedded expression or is the string</span></div><div class="line" id="023253"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023253" label="[02:32:53]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">delimiter.</span></div><div class="line" id="023530"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#023530" label="[02:35:30]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(yes, it changes the lexer rules, not the parser rules)</span></div><div class="line" id="024853"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#024853" label="[02:48:53]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: spot on again. I was referring to runtime memory usage as reported by the kernel.</span></div><div class="line" id="024943"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#024943" label="[02:49:43]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: mksh shows up on guix with a 3.4MB runtime memory usage</span></div><div class="line" id="025113"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#025113" label="[02:51:13]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which interestingly enough is less than the 4MB memory usage reported for kaem</span></div><div class="line" id="025205"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#025205" label="[02:52:05]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah, memory usage, not executable size</span></div><div class="line" id="025548"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025548" label="[02:55:48]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">urgh</span></div><div class="line" id="025553"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025553" label="[02:55:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is painful</span></div><div class="line" id="025607"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025607" label="[02:56:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">byacc has this awk script skel2c</span></div><div class="line" id="025626"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025626" label="[02:56:26]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so we have some pregened .c files made using awk, but we don't have awk when we build byacc</span></div><div class="line" id="025636"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#025636" label="[02:56:36]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Which is sometimes weird to measure, like res column of ps/top lies (like static executables showing at 0 due to not doing any mmap/brk) and I haven't really found one that really seems to work well</span></div><div class="line" id="025850"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#025850" label="[02:58:50]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Maybe <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ibara/yacc">https://github.com/ibara/yacc</a>  (OpenBSD yacc) would be an alternative? It's the yacc I use in my bootstrap-initrd</span></div><div class="line" id="030507"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#030507" label="[03:05:07]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; fossy: argh...</span></div><div class="line" id="030511"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030511" label="[03:05:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hmmmmmm, thanks lanodan, looks promising</span></div><div class="line" id="030617"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#030617" label="[03:06:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; It is only needed for one .y file to build bash...</span></div><div class="line" id="030646"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#030646" label="[03:06:46]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; I guess gash would be another fallback option</span></div><div class="line" id="030701"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#030701" label="[03:07:01]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; But who knows how to properly configure it...</span></div><div class="line" id="030714"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#030714" label="[03:07:14]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; (Outside guix)</span></div><div class="line" id="030937"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030937" label="[03:09:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, gash looked even worse than even mescc to run last time i examined it</span></div><div class="line" id="031417"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#031417" label="[03:14:17]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well interpreted languages with dynamic typing tend to easily hit weird portability problems.</span></div><div class="line" id="031453"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#031453" label="[03:14:53]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and running your scheme program on multiple different scheme platforms to ensure portability seems always to be a skipped step in development.</span></div><div class="line" id="033649"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033649" label="[03:36:49]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmm, might be time for a CI rework soonish</span></div><div class="line" id="033654"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033654" label="[03:36:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">appears KVM is now supported on GH actions</span></div><div class="line" id="033701"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033701" label="[03:37:01]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and per-job time limit is now 6 hrs</span></div><div class="line" id="080123"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080123" label="[08:01:23]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i am *so* confused</span></div><div class="line" id="080136"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080136" label="[08:01:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">coreutils 6.10 was released in Jan 2008</span></div><div class="line" id="080243"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080243" label="[08:02:43]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">however the gnulib import in it includes a module &quot;mgetgroups&quot; which was added to gnulib proper, the commit message says &quot;taken from coreutils&quot;, in Nov 2009</span></div><div class="line" id="080304"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080304" label="[08:03:04]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where was this magic module before that?!</span></div><div class="line" id="080442"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#080442" label="[08:04:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Answer: in a special directory not included in the tarball, only in version control -.-</span></div><div class="line" id="200007"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#200007" label="[20:00:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, digged into TeX implementations available; after patching configure.ac for some hours... tetex-3.0 (year 2006) introduced a dependency against c++ with libxpdf already</span></div><div class="line" id="200141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#200141" label="[20:01:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll give some tetex-1.x from year 1999 a try next... that's round about the same time of ghostscript-7.07 that i salvaged already for tinycc support</span></div><div class="line" id="200414"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#200414" label="[20:04:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the maintainer of tetex gave up almost 20years ago already; and some recent texlive is a nogo (cmake, icu,... c++, and a heavy dependency graph)</span></div><div class="line" id="200737"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#200737" label="[20:07:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i don't know if any compatibility or standard exists, for basic TeX authoring, in any case at best there seems not TeX implementation any later than some tetex-1.x version that could pass acceptence criteria with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="203415"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203415" label="[20:34:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tetex-1.0 got xpdf as dependency on board too, and with it c++</span></div><div class="line" id="203514"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#203514" label="[20:35:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not sure yet, if this can be disected, and tex format be rendered to ps/pdf without xpdf</span></div><div class="line" id="230144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#230144" label="[23:01:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, some old GNU texinfo-4.8 seems to have compiled/linked with tinycc</span></div><br /></div></body></html>